  "seoDescription": "Compute its QR decomposition: factor A into an orthogonal matrix Q and an upper triangular matrix R.",
  "solved": false,
  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Decompose a matrix A into an orthogonal matrix Q and an upper-triangular matrix R using Gram-Schmidt or Householder reflections.\n\nQR factorization solves overdetermined linear systems A x = b via backward substitution on R x = Q^T b without squaring condition numbers.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nA = Q R, \\quad Q^T Q = I, \\quad R_{ij} = 0 \\quad (i > j)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Factor matrix into orthogonal column frame Q and triangular projection coefficients R\n2. Verify orthogonality of Q via Q^T Q = I\n3. Return factorized Q and R component arrays\n\n### Returns\nDictionary containing 'Q' and 'R' matrices.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: QR Decomposition\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of machine learning. Data samples, image pixels, and word embeddings are all represented as multidimensional vectors and matrices. **QR Decomposition** transforms coordinate systems, extracts principal axes of variation, or decomposes complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nQR factorization solves overdetermined linear systems A x = b via backward substitution on R x = Q^T b without squaring condition numbers. In this problem, the goal is to: decompose a matrix a into an orthogonal matrix q and an upper-triangular matrix r using gram-schmidt or householder reflections.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nA = Q R, \\quad Q^T Q = I, \\quad R_{ij} = 0 \\quad (i > j)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Factor matrix into orthogonal column frame Q and triangular projection coefficients R\n2. **Phase 2**: Verify orthogonality of Q via Q^T Q = I\n3. **Phase 3**: Return factorized Q and R component arrays\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Feature Compression & Projections**: Operations like low-rank approximations and orthogonal projections compress high-dimensional feature spaces while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Numerical Stability**: When inverting matrices or solving linear systems, ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with very small determinants or high condition numbers) require regularization to prevent numerical instability.\n* **Vectorized Execution**: Expressing computations as matrix multiplications allows GPUs and CPUs to leverage hardware tensor cores for maximum throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: matrix = [[1.0, 1.0], [1.0, 2.0], [1.0, 3.0]]\n\nOutput: {\"Q\": [[-0.57735, 0.707107], [-0.57735, 0.0], [-0.57735, -0.707107]], \"R\": [[-1.732051, -3.464102], [0.0, -1.414214]]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated la_qr_decomposition under standard test conditions."
